From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:56:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Route the resource, blit and frame paths to the remote Metal
 backend

Extends the remote backend from a control plane to everything a real title
touches. Each addition came from a run naming what it needed, not from
guessing at the surface.

Descriptors and commands travel VERBATIM as their WMT structs wherever they
hold only handles and scalars -- textures, render passes, blit commands. A
parallel schema is what let the earlier pipeline path silently drop blend
state and topology; sending the real struct means a field added upstream
cannot be lost, and an unknown command type is refused WITH ITS NUMBER rather
than guessed at, because a wrong size copies the wrong bytes onto the wire.

Buffers upload only CHANGED 64K pages. The flush was pessimistic by design --
every live buffer in full, before every commit -- which is correct but resends
the same megabytes every frame. Hashing costs a linear read; sending costs a
round trip. The checksum covers every byte, since a missed change appears as a
stale frame rather than an error, and it runs four independent lanes over
64-bit words: a byte-at-a-time hash of every live buffer cost 190ms a frame on
its own, more than the work it was meant to save. Measured 1.05 GB/s against
31.6 GB/s for the same coverage.

Calls whose iOS implementation is a no-op that WRITES ITS OUTPUTS are run
locally rather than guarded. The guard returns without touching outputs that
are not named `ret`, and one such call is made once per frame: a display
version read from uninitialised stack made the renderer reconfigure
presentation several times a second. A call may only be listed local if it
NEVER DEREFERENCES ITS HANDLE on iOS -- three that do were briefly listed and
crashed the instant a window was created, since every handle is a host address
in this mode.

A stall detector names any call over 250ms. All calls are serialised, so one
slow reply stalls the process, and a silent stall is indistinguishable from a
hang.

